{
  "reviewer": "robustness_auditor",
  "paper_id": "bicycle-infrastructure-paper",
  "run_status": "ok",
  "summary": "The paper is descriptive and has no regression, so it has no robustness tables, placebo tests or preregistered subgroup analyses. A search of the full text found no use of \"robust\", \"persists\", \"unchanged\", \"heterogeneity\", \"sensitivity\", \"placebo\", \"subsample\" or \"preregistration\". The relevant remit is therefore the sensitivity support for the validation claims, the full-run counts, the seasonal and regional descriptions, and the listing-data classifications. I report ten findings. The main ones are: (1) the headline claim that LLM–human agreement matches human–human agreement rests on 3 relevant articles out of 34, and the abstract and conclusion omit the caveat that Section 4 gives; (2) the date comparison depends on using the pilot coder as reference; (3) the prompt was revised and the validation set re-run, with no held-out check or pre-update comparison; (4) the broadened-relevance prompt used in the full run was untestable in the pilot, by the authors' own account; (5) run-to-run consistency covers relevance only; (6)–(7) the seasonal-clustering and county-ranking statements are unsupported by any reported figures; (8) the rise in the cycling-mention share is not checked against changing composition or the data gap; (9) the data gap is described inconsistently; (10) the Table 9 classification rule contradicts its own stated examples and Figure 2. The parser-quality guidance was followed: structured table CSV/markdown was not used as primary evidence, and the page text and page images were used instead.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "ROB-001",
      "category": "robustness_overstatement",
      "finding_summary": "The abstract, introduction and conclusion present 97% LLM–human relevance agreement as matching human–human agreement. The comparison rests on only 3 relevant articles among 34, and the abstract and conclusion omit the small-sample caveat that Section 4 gives.",
      "issue_type": "manuscript_issue",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "high",
      "location": {
        "page": 1,
        "page_label": "1",
        "section": "Abstract",
        "text_quote": "the same rate as between two human coders",
        "precision": "exact"
      },
      "claim_text": "On the pilot, the LLM agrees with a human coder on the relevance of 97 percent of the articles, the same rate as between two human coders.",
      "assessment": "partially",
      "cannot_verify_reason": null,
      "evidence_summary": "Tables 3 and 4 show 33 of 34 agreements in each case. 30 of the 34 articles are not relevant for both coders, so most of the 97% comes from easy negatives. Only 3 articles are relevant for the pilot coder. Section 4 says the sample 'is too small to test whether the two agreement rates differ' and that 'the results are thus indicative only', and Section 8 repeats this. The abstract and conclusion state the equality without that qualification. The equality is a descriptive match of two identical-looking tables, not evidence that the LLM is as reliable as a second human. No chance-corrected agreement, precision/recall on the relevant class, or interval is reported.",
      "source_objects": [
        {
          "id": "S1",
          "type": "text",
          "label": "Abstract",
          "path": "work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_001.md",
          "page": 1,
          "page_label": "1",
          "section": "Abstract",
          "text_quote": "the same rate as between two human coders",
          "url": null,
          "object_id": "OBJ-ae6010b8e427194ea28d441e",
          "correspondence": "text",
          "verification_note": null
        },
        {
          "id": "S2",
          "type": "text",
          "label": "Section 4 caveat",
          "path": "work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_007.md",
          "page": 7,
          "page_label": "7",
          "section": "4 Validation",
          "text_quote": "test whether the two agreement rates differ",
          "url": null,
          "object_id": "OBJ-4e6da301f188ae5c7e5d0a63",
          "correspondence": "text",
          "verification_note": null
        }
      ],
      "claim_evidence_links": [
        {
          "claim_text": "LLM–human agreement equals human–human agreement (97%)",
          "source_object_ids": [
            "S1",
            "S2"
          ],
          "relation": "partial",
          "note": "The paper's own validation section says the sample cannot support a comparison of the two rates; the abstract does not carry that qualification."
        }
      ],
      "numeric_check": null,
      "suggested_fix": "Add the small-sample caveat to the abstract and conclusion (for example '3 relevant articles out of 34; indicative only'). Report agreement on the relevant class or a chance-corrected statistic with an uncertainty interval, and avoid implying equivalence."
    },
    {
      "id": "ROB-002",
      "category": "specification_sensitivity",
      "finding_summary": "The claim that the LLM matches the pilot coder's date in all three articles (versus human–human agreement in two of three) depends on treating the pilot coder as the reference. No comparison against the second coder is reported, and the single human–human disagreement is a definitional choice.",
      "issue_type": "manuscript_issue",
      "severity": "low",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "location": {
        "page": 8,
        "page_label": "8",
        "section": "4.2 Agreement between human and LLM",
        "text_quote": "The LLM gives the same date as the pilot coder in all three.",
        "precision": "exact"
      },
      "claim_text": "The LLM gives the same date as the pilot coder in all three; the two human coders, in comparison, agree on two of the three.",
      "assessment": "partially",
      "cannot_verify_reason": null,
      "evidence_summary": "Section 4 states that 'for human–LLM agreement we use the pilot coder as the reference'. The one human–human date disagreement arises because the pilot coder used the official opening and the second coder used the start of the school year. Whether the LLM's date matches the second coder's convention is not reported. The LLM's 3/3 versus the humans' 2/3 therefore cannot be read as a higher-agreement result, because it depends on which coder's convention is the reference. The comparison covers only three articles.",
      "source_objects": [
        {
          "id": "S1",
